  {
    "group": "Dementation",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Paranoia",
    "flavor": "The vampire may stir the sensory centers of the victim's brain, flooding the victim's senses with visions, scents, sounds or feelings that are not really there. These are only fleeting glimpses of these senses, barely perceptible to the victim. The vampire cannot control which ones the subject feels. The \"haunting\" effect may take the form of the subjects repressed fears, guilty memories or the like. The effects are never pleasant or unobtrusive. The victim may very well feel she is going mad, or that the world around her is.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Dementation subject's Wits",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dementation",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "This lasts for 1 hour per success.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire using this power becomes irrationally paranoid with the constant feeling of being watched and manipulated.",
    "failureText": "The subject is unaffected, but becomes aware of the vampires icy cold presence.",
    "successText": "ROLL RESULTS",
    "notes": "Success: Successes vary by subject. It might be minuses to Dominion rolls, sudden Virtue or Vice attempts, open to suggestion or manipulation, and so on. The victim is simply haunted by the false things being fed to their senses, and will have no composure or concentration.\n\nDramatic Success: The Malkavian twists the victim's own mind into questioning itself, doubting its own perceptions and knowledge, and rotting its own stability through anxiety. It starts with something as subtle as being unable to find one's keys, and progresses through the kind of creeping doubt that makes one wonder things like, \"Didn't this room used to be red? I'm sure it was red,\" and, didn't leave that out — who's been in here?\" Eventually, the victim may not be sure if he is being haunted, going crazy, or just being fucked with, but he will have no doubt that something is very wrong. Not only does the victim hear and see things, but they also doubt things that are actually there as well. The victim cannot make any assured action before making Wits roll. If they succeed they can continue to act as they intended. If they fail, they doubt something about their actions and must act differently and to a different end/goal/result. If they botch they gain the Flaw: Madness at 1 dot.",
    "sourcePage": 172
  },
  {
    "group": "Dementation",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Voice of Madness",
    "flavor": "By merely addressing his victims aloud, the vampire can drive targets into fits of blind rage or fear, forcing them to abandon blind reason or higher thought. Victims are plagued by hallucinations of their subconscious demons, and try to flee or destroy their subconscious demons. Tragedy almost always follows in the wake of this power's use.",
    "vitaeCost": 4,
    "costText": "4 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Persuasion + Dementation minus target's Resolve ; Roll separately for each victim",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dementation",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve ; Roll separately for each victim",
    "actionText": "10 seconds of barely coherent ranting and shouting of pure madness to gain everyone's attention, then followed by 1 round/second of muttering, calculating, and punctuated mad shouts for as long as the Malkavian wants the effect to go on or until someone finally silences them. The mad gibbering does not prevent the Malkavian from doing any actions they desire except for any form of communication. They may only talk in mad riddles and may not even use hand gestures to give commands or answers. Once the Malkavian stops ranting, the effects last for a number of rounds/seconds more equal to his Blood Potency.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire using this power summons the voices and illusions of his own demons and is the only one to suffer the effects of this power for 1 minute per botch die.",
    "failureText": "A few moments of awkward stares at your strange outburst, nothing happens.",
    "successText": "All victims (for enemy, ally, and oblivious innocent alike) within hearing range (simply covering of ears or such will not aid them in blocking out the madman's voice) of the vampire's voice and able to actively listen to them immediately fly into an impassioned lashing out at their false perceptions, speaking aloud their hate and denial of their innermost fears or failings, or acting out irrationally their metal derangements.\n\nSometimes they simply flee in terror, sometimes they attack all those around them in a blind rage, sometimes they shout, argue, or use powers on their imaginings or others. The madness they see and feel is often projected onto people and items in the area around them, though sometimes more rarely, they will lash out at the air where a perceived fear or hate is in their mind's eye.",
    "notes": "ROLL RESULTS",
    "sourcePage": 173
  },
  {
    "group": "Dementation",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "Passion",
    "flavor": "The vampire may stir his victim's emotions; either heightening them to a fevered pitch or blunting them till the target is completely desensitized. In this way a vampire can turn a mild irritation into a frothing rage or true love into casual interest.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma Dementation — subject's Wits",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dementation",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire using this power becomes irrationally irate over his inability to manipulate his target and must make a Dominion roll needing 1 success to stay calm.",
    "failureText": "The subject is unaffected, but becomes aware of the vampires icy cold presence.",
    "successText": "The subject has its current emotion moved right or left one space per success for a duration of 1 hour per success. Or up or down for 2 successes (only may go up or down a single time), and if changed up or down you may then send them left or right one space per further success. See chart below:\n\nAnger Indifferent Frustrated Angry Enraged Berserk",
    "notes": "Sadness Relaxed Apathetic Sad Depressed Distraught\n\nJealousy Accepting Envious Jealous Resentfu I Demanding\n\nLust Disinterested ttracted Lustful Desirous Forceful\n\nLove Cordial Infatuated In Love Passionate Mad Love\n\nHappiness Content Amused Happy Elated Ecstatic\n\nExceptional Success: The subject has no ability to fight the emotion welling up within them, and may not spend a point of Willpower to resist.",
    "sourcePage": 173
  },
  {
    "group": "Dementation",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "23",
    "flavor": "This discipline is reserved for only the most vengeful and influential of oracles. Not only has the oracle seen the visions of what is to come or gleaned hidden secrets — but they have decided action must be taken on them. Perhaps a catastrophe is to be avoided, or perhaps some great boon can be gotten if only the right buttons of Fate are pressed. This power allows the Oracle to send a person in their stead to investigate these connections — willing or othemvise. They do this by infecting the target with a fractured bit of their own mind, making them obsessed with finding the hidden clues and agendas the Malkavian has set them on.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "2 Vitae + 1 Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "At least a few minutes of the Malkavian making madcap demands of a person who is in some way paying attention to the Malkavian and within 30ft. A same person may only be put under Geas once per lunar cycle (approx. once every 30 days)",
    "duration": "The duration of the Geas is until the same time the next night plus an additional night per point of additional Vitae spent.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "Your character suffers a painful migraine which gives the character perfect clarity into their own psychosis and forces them to panic and wallow in self-doubt and misery as they realize how broken their mind is, only to feel this perfect sanity slip away in the next few minutes when they return to their normal selves.",
    "failureText": "Your shouted demands come across as laughable and easily dismissed.",
    "successText": "ROLL RESULTS",
    "notes": "Success: This spell creates a Geas, a forced duty so to speak, enabling a Malkavian to make another person obsessed with obscure correlations to complete a series of small tasks or work towards a specific goal. The Malkavian is under no obligation to be reasonable when levying a Geas upon another, although the objective must still be possible. Nor will this power allow the character to commit suicide or do major harm (cutting off fingers or stabbing out eyes, shooting a loved one, or such).\n\nbetter use of this power is to get others to go on your insane investigations that have arisen from your visions. Get others to look into things, get information, meet dubious contacts, and steal gems or the like for you. Note that, unlike the powers of Dominate, this spell does not force a target to perform a task. It instead sets up terrible repercussions if he doesn't perform it.\n\nThose who violate the terms of a Geas (failing to actively work toward fulfilling the Geas counts as violating it) during the discipline's duration; suffer the effects of the \"Evil Eye ' described below, until such time as the Geas would normally expire. Additionally the victim suffers the Malkavian Shattered Mind effect that the character was suffering at the time this Discipline was used for the duration of the Geas.\n\nLastly, at any time the Malkavian interacts with a person under a Geas they gave them, the person will tell the Malkavian (willing or not; likely with tortured flustration) their progress on the Geas, any difficulties their encounter, anything preventing them from easily accomplishing the task and anything they have done to sabotage or undermine the task.\n\nDramatic Success: The subject also gains a 1 point Madness of the Malkavian's choice for the duration of the Geas.\n\nThe Evil Eye This is a basic curse intended to bring about immediate ill fortune upon another. In ancient times, such a working was almost always undertaken by way of physically staring at the one to be cursed, directing a baleful glare at him and thereby hexing his actions. Now, it may be directed through less obvious means, but many Malkavians still prefer the old-fashioned method. The Evil Eye is not life threatening, but merely able to ruin their best efforts and possibly allow them to fail delicate actions. It could make someone's moming commute a waking nightmare, causing him to get splashed by a passing car, harassed by an utter nutcase on the bus, and defecated on by a low-flying pigeon as he walks in the door 45 minutes late.\n\nOr it could be something like being struck in an already wounded leg or getting in trouble with a woman's large boyfriend while hitting on her in a club. Or it could be to see their fears around any corner or to have visions and nightmares of things they find sorrowful or painful. Or any mix of things as the nights go on... This does not guarantee failure or harm, but it largely increases it. The system for this is Botch dice. Any time a person suffering the Evil Eye rolls dice, all I-7's are treated as Botch dice, and create creative and only mildly harmful complications. Drawing a Red Joker is treated as a Black Joker and a Black Joker is treated as majorly harmful, or greatly debilitating or a major loss.",
    "sourcePage": 174
  },
  {
    "group": "Dementation",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Eyes of Chaos",
    "flavor": "This peculiar power allows the vampire to take advantage of the fractured wisdom hidden in insanity. She may scrutinize the hidden \"patterns\" of a person's soul, the convolutions of a vampire s inner nature, or even seemingly random events in nature itself. The vampire with this power can discern the most well-hidden psychoses or gain insights into a person's true self or events surrounding them. A vampire using this power can see the hidden truths in the chaotic patterns around them. Thus it is common that vampires with this power will be attracted to events that are out of control, and when such situations are lacking, they will not hesitate to create some chaos of their own.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Investigation (for supernatural stuff) or Streetwise (for natural stuff) + Dementation (The difficulty in interpreting the visions can be modified as per the situation, ability to focus on subject and knowledge of su bject.",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Investigation",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dementation",
    "actionText": "Turn of concentration",
    "duration": "The duration is as long as the vampire can go undisturbed and focus on the pattern they wish to discern.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire using this power becomes aware of hidden patterns and agendas and connections as described below, except they are all patently false. And he gets an automatic number of successes equal to the number of botch die he rolled in order to convince others of this falsehood.",
    "failureText": "A few moments of awkward contemplation, nothing happens.",
    "successText": "And additional dice can be gained by performing ritualistic rites or having ancient relics or possibly even due to story and character relations. These bonus dice are typically from + I to 43 depending on how significant they are)",
    "notes": "Success: The vampire gets a vision of esoteric events detailing out the truth they discerned within the chaos. The vision is vague enough to be interpretive, but it is full of enough alliteration and metaphor and symbolism to reveal the truth... if interpreted correctly. A Malkavian's Shattered Mind Flaw will relate to the visions they see when using Eyes of Chaos. Thus the threads of fate they do successfully tie together are always told through the gibberish of their Shattered Mind Flaw.\n\nThe timeframe that these visions can depict is up to 5 min per success.\n\nNOTE: Each use of this power in a single night increases the characters' Madness. Each use after the first gives the character cumulative -1 to all actions as they become more and more dysfunctionally insane and distracted by their Shattered Mind effects, or to suffer those effects even if not bloodline Malkavian. Subjects and Actions that Eyes of Chaos can work with: Right Place, Right Time: The character enters a scene looking for someone who might be there or they are looking for a specific event to happen locally at the moment they arrive, or maybe they are wanting to be able to overhear the right part of the conversation they are eavesdropping on. This is for events, not items.\n\nMissed Me! : The character is moving in combat and is just so happens to see the right way to duck, dodge, dive, tumble, roll or run. This gives bonus/success to Defense.\n\nIt's Over Here: When the character is looking for clues, hoping to find hidden item or secret door, they find it, or a person hiding (does not work against anyone using Obfuscate discipline)\n\nYour Mistress Says Hi: The character can see the tell-tale signs and series of events between two or three people and know a bit of what they are up to. This gives character detai s on actions other characters took even when they were not there for a given event.\n\nYou Know You Want To: This is the same as 'Your Mistress Says Hi,' except it gives details on a planned event a character is going to participate in. This might give you details on a business meeting to happen later that day, or a drug deal later that night, or an informer running back to tell his boss what he witnessed. The event must be planned.\n\nLittle Birdy Told Me... : The character knows one bit of truth about character or item. It's just a simple bit of detail that gives insight or intent into the subject. Or they can know an individual's dots (if any) in any Skill or Attribute if they relate to the scene in a significant way. Or they can detect and know the details of a person suffering supernatural mind control, spiritual possession or alterations of personality and sanity.\n\nNo, Play It Backwards: The character can determine the message in a coded missive or a mysterious c ue's purpose or the intentions of a hidden puppet master or cause of a mysterious event.\n\nThe amount of detail received for each: Roll Result Botch The character not only gains the wrong information, but they consider it to be totally correct. On any next actions in that scene they must act on this wrong information and attempt to make use of it even if given contradictory facts/info. Zero Nothing comes to mind..\n\nSuccess They gain a vague insight/gut feeling/detail/info Successes They gain a vague mental image plus vague insight/gut feeling/detail/info Successes They gain a highly detailed mental image plus vague insight/gut feeling/detail/info",
    "sourcePage": 175
  },
  {
    "group": "Dominate",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Command",
    "flavor": "Once he has established eye contact, the vampire issues a single, one-word command that must be obeyed instantly. The order must be clear and straightforward — freeze, jump, run, stop, fall, cough, blink and so forth. If the command is ambiguous or confusing, the subject might respond slowly or perform the task poorly. Further, commands are always interpreted (within reason) in the subject's best interests. For example, if the victim stands on the edge of a roof and the character commands \"Jump!\" the victim might jump up and down, rather than leap off the roof. Of course, the victim would probably not leap off the roof even if the character could find a way to order it. Subjects of Command cannot be made to directly harm themselves, so an obviously suicidal order such as \"Die!\" is ineffective. ' 'Sleep\" and the like causes the subject to follow the order only if she does not believe herself to be in any mortal danger.\n\ncharacter seduced by a Vampire might sleep if so instructed, but one in the middle of a combat or interrogation certainly does not. In such situations, the character merely falls senseless, but only for a turn. The vampire may include the command word in sentence, in order to conceal her use of the power from others. The command itself must be stressed, and the character must make eye contact precisely when that word is spoken. Observers may notice the unusual emphasis, but only the most alert — and those familiar with the Discipline — are likely to realize what has occurred.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Intelligence 4 Dominate minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Intelligence",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dominate",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The subject does not obey and is immune to the character's Dominate until the next sunset.",
    "failureText": "The character loses or ties the contested roll and the subject does not obey.",
    "successText": "The character wins the contested roll by getting the most successes, and the victim obeys literally and with appreciable self-preservation.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "The character wins the contested roll with five or more successes, and the victim not only obeys but rationalizes what she does as her own decision until and unless someone questions her about it in depth. Assuming the commanded action is one that can carried out for some time, such as \"wait\" or \"sleep,\" the subject obeys for a number of turns equal to the successes obtained on the Command roll. If this power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has a blood tie, a +2 bonus applies to the user's roll.",
    "sourcePage": 176
  },
  {
    "group": "Dominate",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Mesmerize",
    "flavor": "The source of many legends of the vampire's hypnotic gaze, Mesmerize allows the Vampire to implant a false thought or suggestion into the subject's subconscious mind. The power requires not only eye contact but intense concentration and precise wording, so both the character and the subject must be free from distraction. The Vampire may activate the imposed thought or command immediately —\"Walk over here and open the door\" — or he may establish a stimulus that activates the suggestion at a later date — \"When you see a man in a blue suit with a red rose in his lapel, you will spill your drink on him.\" The victim must be able to understand the vampire. The Vampire must maintain eye contact only as long as it takes to implant the suggestion or idea. Mesmerize can deliver truly complex and long-term commands, such as following someone, taking notes on her activities and reporting back on the first of the next month. A subject can have only one suggestion implanted at any given time.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Expression + Dominate minus target's Wits",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Expression",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dominate",
    "opposedBy": "target's Wits",
    "actionText": "Instant, if this power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has a blood tie, a +2 bonus applies.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The subject does not obey and is immune to the character's Dominate until the next sunset.",
    "failureText": "The character loses or ties the contested roll and the subject does not obey.",
    "successText": "The character wins the contested roll by getting the most successes, and the victim obeys to the best of his ability. If the character wins the contested roll with five or more successes. The victim not only obeys, but rationalizes what she does as her own decision until and unless someone questions her about it in depth. This power lasts for as long as it takes the subject to carry out the required task, or until the character is destroyed or enters torpor. Impossible actions such as, \"Count every grain of sand on this beach,\" automatically fail to take root in the subject's mind.\n\nWhen using Dominate, short of taking weeks to Condition your victim to talk to you in truth and confidence, you can demand confessions with Mesmerize. This works as normal Mesmerize roll, but instead of giving a simple command, you are asking a simple question. The question must be short and able to be answered with a 'yes' or 'no' or a one word or name response. You get as many questions as you have dot sin Blood Potency. After that each additional question allows the victim to make a Wits + Resolve roll needing more successes than you got on your Mesmerize roll to shake themselves from the trance. Regardless of rolls, this questioning cannot take more than 1 minute per dot in Blood Potency you have.",
    "notes": "Extended Command Actions If, during the course of carrying out the implanted suggestion, the victim realizes he put himself in danger or acts completely contrary to his normal moral code, a Resolve roll may be made to shake off the compulsion. This is a contested roll. Successes rolled must exceed the number of successes obtained for the dominating character when the individual was first mesmerized. Dramatic Failure: Not only must the subject continue to carry out the command, no more Resolve rolls may be made to shake the dominator's control, no matter what the subject is forced to do. Failure: The victim must continue to carry out the command as ordered. Success: The victim escapes the suggestion. Exceptional Success: The victim defies the suggestion, and the dominator incurs a -1 penalty to future attempts to Dominate him until the next night. No matter how strong the dominator's will is or how many successes he obtains, he cannot make a subject harm himself directly.\n\nAny command to commit suicide is therefore ignored, although commands that are likely to lead to harm — such as, \"Walk into that crack house and shoot the man in the red shirt\" — are enforceable, subject to the Resolve rolls discussed above. If a vampire attempts to Mesmerize a subject who is already under the influence of a previously implanted directive, compare the successes rolled in the attempt against the successes gained during the implantation of the first suggestion.\n\nIf the character obtains more successes than the previous Vampire, the new command may supplant the old one. If he does not, the original command remains active and the new one fails to take root. In case of a tie, the original command takes precedent over the new one. A vampire seeking to supplant an old suggestion with a new one must also acquire successes in excess of those rolled for the subject in his contested Resolve roll.",
    "sourcePage": 177
  },
  {
    "group": "Dominate",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "The Forgetful Mind",
    "flavor": "vampire with this power can literally delve into a subject's mind, stealing or reshaping memories at whim. The power, as with all uses of Dominate, requires eye contact. The Forgetful Mind does not allow for telepathic contact.\n\nInstead, the vampire acts much like a hypnotist, asking direct questions to draw answers from the subject, and then describing in detail any new memories she wishes to impose on the victim. Simple alterations, such as blu1Ting brief and recent memories, are easy enough (and very effective for eliminating evidence of feeding or other Masquerade breaches).\n\nMore comprehensive alterations, up to and including a complete reconstruction of the victim's past and even identity, are possible albeit substantially more difficult. The victim's subconscious struggles to resist false memories implanted from without, so the degree to which the vampire details new memories has direct bearing on how fully the subject assimilates them.\n\nAn incomplete or simplistic false memory shatters much more swiftly and easily than does one with more attention to detail. For instance, ' 'You drove home after work and had a very bad evening\" is not likely to hold up. Far more effective would be an account such as, \"You left work late, due to a last-minute change ordered by the client.\n\nYou still managed to get stuck in rush hour for an extra 25 minutes, and you were seriously flustrated by the time you pulled off the freeway. There weren't even any good songs on the radio. Three blocks from home, a cop pulled you over for coasting through a stop sign. At least he only gave you a warning, but it was still just one more hassle.\n\nThe frozen dinner you microwaved was awful, the TV movie starred some has been you didn't like, and the news was depressing. You finally turned off the TV in disgust and went to bed.\"\n\nTruly effective use of The Forgetful Mind, then, requires substantial finesse and patience, as well as the ability to carefully and thoroughly think things through ahead of time. It's fairly simple to sift through a victim's memories and strip out recent events without even knowing precisely what happened, but doing so leaves a gap in the memory that can lead to further problems.\n\nMost people aren't comfortable realizing they've lost a few hours or a night's worth of memories, and they're likely to try to find out what happened. Even new memories that the character imposes rarely have the same strength as the originals, and they can be broken or at least contradicted through investigation. For instance, a victim might not recall that she was attacked by a vampire, but she might remember being bitten, chalking it up to an animal attack or a spider. More vivid memories can return as snippets in dreams or be triggered by an unusual olfactory stimulus or spoken phrase.\n\nThe process might take weeks or even years before the victim can begin to make sense of these flashes, but even the most effective memory manipulation is far from perfect — or truly permanent memories, however.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae per roll",
    "dicePoolText": "Persuasion + Dominate minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dominate",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Extended (1-10+ successes, based on the detail and complexity of memory; each roll represents five minutes of mental manipulation)",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The attempt fails, and the subject is immune to the character's Dominate until the next sunset. All accumulated successes are lost and the subject continues to remember what happened.",
    "failureText": "The attempt fails.\n\nSuggested Modifiers",
    "successText": "The character makes headway toward altering a block of memories.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "The character makes considerable headway toward altering a block of memories. Even a single success pacifies the victim for the length of time it takes to complete the process. Both vampire and subject must be safe and relatively composed when this power is used; it cannot be used to calm someone already in combat, for example. To restore stolen or false memories, or to sense when memories have been altered, a character must possess Dominate at a level equal to or greater than that of the vampire who first tampered with the subject's mind. If such is the case, the player then makes a contested roll in each stage of the extended action, to be compared against the initial user's attempt, and must score more successes than the predecessor did.",
    "notes": "ROLL RESULTS\n\nModifier Situation +2 Power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has a blood tie. to -3 The character's description of the new memories is lacking in detail.",
    "sourcePage": 178
  },
  {
    "group": "Dominate",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "Conditioning",
    "flavor": "Through constant veiled whispers, subtle hints and frequent sustained manipulation, a vampire can slowly render a particular victim substantially more suggestible. Conditioning is normally used only on favored or valuable servants and, over sufficient time, makes the character's efforts to Dominate a subject far easier while making it harder for any other Vampire to do so. Acquiring such complete control over even the weakest mind is no easy task, however, and it normally takes weeks or even months to accomplish. While they are undeniably loyal, servants subject to Condi tioning lose much of their ability to think for themselves, their own personalities blotted out by the will imposed on them. They follow their orders literally and to the letter, rarely showing any imagination or judgment, or taking the initiative to act on their own. Some vampires have remarked that, after a sufficiently lengthy period of Conditioning, their servants actually resemble the walking dead more so than their masters.",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Dominate minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dominate",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Extended, each roll may be performed once per day",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The victim's mind is shattered with all of your undead desires, and your vampire can hear their fragmented thoughts in their head, make a Stains roll now for the honor you have wrought upon this mortal.",
    "failureText": "All of the user's successes to date are negated and the subject is immune to the character's Dominate until the next sunset.",
    "successText": "The victim is how utterly subservient to your vampire. Their desires match your desires, their goals match your goals. They will retain all of their own memories, but any attachments or ideals counter to yours are considered to be completely unacceptable. All commands or use of Dominate on a Conditioned victim are automatically successful after the cost is spent. Additionally, you may implant triggered actions into your Conditioned servant, that with a clear and obvious reason — will cause your Conditioned servant to petfotm an action you implanted into their subconscious as best they are able. However, during the time they are Conditioned, the victim's Intelligence is temporarily reduced by 1 dot, to a minimum of 0. If this brings them to O, they are a dull witted and slow automaton who can only do the most simple of tasks — poorly.",
    "notes": "Mortals, ghouls, other vampires and other supernatural creatures are all subject to Conditioning. Those who are already under a state of Conditioned or Possessed or otherwise not acting of their own mind are not valid targets for this discipline.\n\nCost: 1 Vitae + 1 Willpower per roll\n\nConditioning does not have any mechanical effect until five more successes are accumulated in excess of the victim's Willpower dots. (If the subject has Willpower 6, Conditioning begins to take effect once II successes are achieved.) Once this benchmark has been reached, the subject is now Conditioned.\n\nDuration: A number of weeks equal to your Blood Potency plus 1 week per success.",
    "sourcePage": 179
  },
  {
    "group": "Dominate",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Possession",
    "flavor": "By this point, the Vampire need not rely on spoken commands and limited understanding. With the mere locking of the victim's gaze, the vampire can utterly supplant the subject's own psyche with her own, possessing the subject like a malevolent spirit.\n\nOnce the Vampire has crushed the victim's will, she literally takes over the body, inhabiting it and controlling it as easily as she does her own. The mortal enters something of a fugue state while possessed, and he is aware of events only in a dreamlike, distorted fashion.\n\nThe Vampire suffers the opposite effect. Her mind now resides within the victim, and her own body falls into a torpor-like state, becoming indistinguishable from a true corpse. The vampire may choose to break the possession and return to her body at any time, over any distance, but until and unless she does so, her true physical form is utterly helpless.\n\nVampires cannot possess other Vampires in this fashion, regardless of force of will or differences in Blood Potency. Mortals alone are subject to possession.",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Dominate minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Dominate",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Extended, The vampire locks eyes with the victim and begins the process of utterly stripping away the subject's Willpower. The vampire must obtain a number of successes in excess of the victim's Temporary Willpower. The victim is held, trapped in this psychic struggle, as long as the vampire's player continues to win each contested roll.",
    "duration": "A number of days equal to your Blood Potency plus I Day per point of Willpower spent after the first.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire's beast rises up and rages at the defiance of this mortal. Your character loses a number of Temporary Willpower equal to the number of Botch dice. The attempt is over and the victim is immune to that vampire's Dominate until the next sunset.",
    "failureText": "The attempt is over and the victim is immune to that vampire's Dominate until the next sunset.",
    "successText": "ROLL RESULTS",
    "notes": "Stigmata Any injuries inflicted on the subject also affect the vampire's body. If the subject dies while the vampire is still present, the Vampire falls into torpor immediately. (Some believe the soul attempts to find its way back to its own body during this time.) If the Vampire's physical form is destroyed, she remains trapped in the mortal body for a year and a day, at which point her psyche is lost to oblivion and is unrecoverable. Any attempt to \"re-Embrace\" a vampire's spirit trapped in a host body results only in Final Death.\n\nWhen finally freed of vampiric possession, some mortals recover immediately, while others lie comatose or suffer trauma-induced psychoses for days or even weeks before recovering. Because a vampire experiences everything her physical body does tasting food, soaking up the sun many become addicted to the sensations and spend more time possessing mortals than inhabiting their own bodies.\n\nIt is possible, though uncommon, for a vampire to neglect her physical body long enough for it to starve into torpor while she's \"out.\" If a vampire's dormant body slips or is forced into torpor, the vampire's spirit automatically returns to its body.\n\nCost: 1 Willpower\n\nSuccess: Your vampire takes possession of the mortal's body. The character may take any actions she chooses, travel as far from her own body as she wishes, and any Disciplines she has (acting as if her host was herself for their use).\n\nShe may continue to use this power during daylight while possessing the subject, but she must still force herself to stay awake during the day as per Daytime rules. The vampire may choose to end the possession and return to her body at any time, regardless of distance. This occurs automatically if the vampire fails to remain awake.",
    "sourcePage": 180
  },
  {
    "group": "Du'l-Faqar",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Hassassin",
    "flavor": "Stories of the ancient assassins of the desert kingdoms whisper of the ghul, nocturnal creatures that could stalk their prey across sands and shadow. The Al-Haqqadi draw upon these legends, becoming their modern embodiment — fearsome, silent, and unstoppable.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae per Technique",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "The vampire may activate multiple of these in a scene.",
    "successText": "Each one may only be activated once, and they may only have as many active as they have dots in Blood Potency. Each technique has a different Duration.\n\nHatamah That which Breaks to Pieces",
    "notes": "Duration: I Attack, once used, it is no longer active.\n\nResults: Vitae spent will infuse the assassins will into their body, making their physical form stronger than steel. 1 point of Vitae spent and a weapon that does Bashing damage which strikes your character is shattered and no damage is taken. 3 points of Vitae spent will ignore damage from a weapon that does Lethal damage and shatter that weapon (this includes projectiles like a bullet). Against body parts, like fists, flaws, feet, etc — this discipline prevents any non-Potence damage and does a point of Lethal to the body part that someone his them with. Any weapon or attack that does Aggravated damage ignores this discipline altogether. is common in Arabic phraseology.\n\nGhadab Wrath\n\nDuration: I Combat Scene\n\nResults: When a creature in a state of Dominion attacks the assassin, the assassin gains their Wits dots as bonus Defense against the Beast-driven creature. And they gain their Intelligence dots as a bonus to any attacks against the Beast-driven creature.\n\nIstishhäd Martyrdom\n\nDuration: 1 Combat Scene\n\nResults: Each attack that does Lethal or worse damage to the assassin regains your character 1 Willpower point.\n\nSakinah Divine Tranquility\n\nDuration: I Combat Scene\n\nResults: When in combat the character is divinely tranquil, able to see the perfect flow of the dance of death around them. All I's (botch dice) count as 10's for them during this scene. Additionally they cannot accidentally or be forced into Dominion by any means. When not in combat the assassin additionally gets their Wits dots to detect any attempt to take them by surprise or unaware.",
    "sourcePage": 181
  },
  {
    "group": "Du'l-Faqar",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Alsahwa",
    "flavor": "The Al-Haqqadi do not merely drink blood; they consume the essence of life itself, drawing forth the latent knowledge hidden in every droplet. This power, like the djinn of old, allows them to see through the eyes of another, to feel what another felt, to become the very breath of the city.\n\n'From the taste of the wind, I can find my enemy, they recite, recalling the words of Shahmaran, the Serpent Queen, who once spoke to a young king and taught him how to follow a foe's shadow across a thousand leagues. The Al-Haqqadi taste the blood and know the streets, the paths of the living mapped in their minds like a painted tapestry. They hunt with the knowledge of the ghul that stalks the deep desert, sensing every heartbeat, every whisper. The blood becomes their guide",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae + 1 Willpower (The vampire must first consume one Vitae from another source (vampire or mortal). )",
    "dicePoolText": "Perception -k Streetwise",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Perception",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Streetwise",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "A botched roll floods the vampire with random memories from those whose vitae she has ingested in the past, during which time she is stunned and completely unable to act until she spends blood in an attempt to purge the visions (one Vitae per failure on the roll which may require more than one turn to spend, depending on Generational limits).",
    "failureText": "Nothing is seen and this blood is wasted. Another attempt may not be made again that night.",
    "successText": "The number of successes determines the clarity accessed from senses and experiences echoed in another's blood which the vampire has consumed. As the visions manifest, the vampire may lose track of reality though an attack to their person will snap the out of this power and back to their self in an instant. Consult the chart below for effects.",
    "notes": "Successes Effect\n\n1 Vague impressions from the most recent interactions and senses of the person you fed on up to a few hours before the blood was consumed. It is as if you were standing behind their eyes during that time. During this observation your vampire is unaware of their own surroundings, so much to they have to concentrate to understand what they are seeing in the impressions.\n\n4 Vague impressions from the most recent interactions and senses of the person you fed on, and anyone they interacted with or were very near to, up to the beginning of the night their blood was consumed. It is as if you were standing behind their eyes during that time, and you can switch from one to the other at any time, but the connection extends no further to anyone other than these initial meetings. Your vampire can still keep their senses and wits about them while observing these impressions.\n\nPelfect vison and senses of anyone the person you fed on, as well as anyone they interacted with up to the beginning of the previous night. You can switch to anyone they interacted with at any time, and may then switch to anyone that person interacted with — up to a number of people removed from the first equal to your Blood Potency. Your vampire is the wind which flows through the city. While they are observing these visions, they are utterly unaware of their own surroundings.",
    "sourcePage": 182
  },
  {
    "group": "Du'l-Faqar",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "Nansaa",
    "flavor": "No memory holds the Al-Haqqadi for long. Tales of the djinn who could vanish like smoke on the wind, leaving only the faintest trace, speak to this ancient power. It is said in the Kitab al-Jinniyat, \"The unseen is more real than the seen; the hidden truth more lasting than the spoken word. \"People who meet them forget the details, or they invent stories to explain away the strange sensations left in the Al-Haqqadi's wake. Memories twist, falter, and eventually crumble to dust in the minds of those they encounter, the truth lost in a haze of half-remembered impressions. People who meet an Al-Haqqadi are unable to give testimony of having seen or interacted with one.",
    "costText": "Special - This power costs nothing if you are Al- Haqqadi.",
    "dicePoolText": "Anyone who has met you who is not Al-Haqqadi must roll Wits + Perception minus your dots in Blood Potency to remember vague details of your meeting.",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Wits",
    "opposedBy": "your dots in Blood Potency to remember vague details of your meeting",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Special - This power is permanently on if you are Al-Haqqadi. It lasts 1 scene.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "If the person trying to remember you Botches their roll, they forget meeting you entirely and their mind creates fiction to fill in the blank of that timeframe.",
    "failureText": "If the person trying to remember you fails their roll - they remember you, but cannot bring themselves to tell anyone or give any details. The words fail them, and their mind cannot produce any way to convey this meeting.",
    "successText": "If you are not, it costs 1 Vitae + 1 Willpower",
    "notes": "Success: If the person trying to remember you makes their roll they remember you, and can recall you the meeting but the details are vague.\n\nSome powers which can draw out memories, especially ones that can draw out forgotten, or hidden memories can defeat this discipline, so long as they get more successes on their roll than the user of this power has in Blood Potency.",
    "sourcePage": 183
  },
  {
    "group": "Du'l-Faqar",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "Naqash Shahidaan",
    "flavor": "This power draws on the ancient rites of summoning djinn to compel truth. Stories of long-dead scholars who summoned the jinn to answer questions of the soul are woven into this discipline. The Al-Haqqadi pour the vitae into a basin, echoing the ancient words of the Akkadian necromancer-priests, who used blood to draw spirits from the underworld. 'Blood is the ink of the soul, they whisper, \"and the truth is written in crimson. \"Each drop of vitae resonates with the life it once carried, and the Al-Haqqadi stir this essence to awaken the spirit of the blood. Lies unravel like threads pulled from a tapestry, revealing the hidden truth behind every word spoken. The blood does not lie...",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "See below",
    "actionText": "Time it takes to pour blood into container.",
    "duration": "This power lasts for a number of questions equal to the questioner's Blood Potency or until the questioner stops touching the blood.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "See results below",
    "failureText": "See results below",
    "successText": "To use this power, the vampire must have one pint of the target's blood, which she pours into an open container capable of holding the fluid indefinitely, such as a basin or goblet. The container must have dimensions that permit her to, at the least, dip one finger into the vitae for the duration of the questioning. The blood reduces until spent, gradually smoldering into a thin red mist that dissipates entirely to vapor by the scene's conclusion.\n\nFor each statement made by the target that the judge wishes to examine, the target rolls Wits + Subterfuge minus your vampire's Resolve (the target may spend Willpower as normal, the judge may not). Successes on their roll indicate the degree of truth or falsehood that the questioner becomes aware of. You may not ask the same question twice, but if you make a Manipulation + Subterfuge roll to re-word your same question such that the target does not realize it.",
    "notes": "Successes Amount gleaned from question\n\nsuccess You are utterly uncertain if the target's statement was true or false, and may not even re-word your question to try again.\n\n2-4 successes Know the truth as the target understands it, but no knowledge if this is the actual truth or not.\n\nsuccess Know if the target's statements actual truth or not, including information the target remembers, but does not understand. And know if they think they are telling the truth or not (or even a partial truth).\n\nFail Same as below, as well as information that the target is not conscious y aware of but was near enough to have potentially learned, or has lost due to supernatural influence like Dominate.\n\nBotch The target gains no more roll, all further questions are as Fail above.",
    "sourcePage": 183
  },
  {
    "group": "Du'l-Faqar",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Udhiyah",
    "flavor": "This ultimate power, whose name means \"sacrifice,\" is steeped in the grim legends of ghuls who fed upon the dead in the darkness beyond the campfire's glow. The Al-Haqqadi remember the story of the Black Ghul, who, with a single touch, could drain the life from a man and leave only a shadow.\n\nTo wield Udhiyah is to become the embodiment of that ancient terror. A single stroke to the neck cuts more than flesh; it severs the thread of life itself, and no magic or blood can bind it back together until the rising of the sun. \"By the name of the One who Split the Moon,\" they chant, \"let the blood flow.\" The wound remains open, untouched by time or healing, a mark of judgment that only the sun's first light can absolve.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Single attack, results last until sun rises",
    "successText": "A successful cut to the neck of a victim cannot heal by any supernatural means (or until the rising of the sun that day should they live that long). They bleed out in a number of seconds equal to their Stamina if unattended, and in a number of minutes if the throat is simply staunched by hand or similar (Fortitude multiples each time to bleed out. i.e. Fortitude at I doubles time till they bleed out.).",
    "sourcePage": 184
  },
  {
    "group": "Majesty",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Awe",
    "flavor": "This power makes the user seem exponentially more charismatic and magnetic than he normally is. The force of his personality issues forth in waves, drawing people to him like moths to a flame. Perhaps the greatest use for Awe is its ability to facilitate public speaking or debate. Whatever the vampire says, people are likely to lend serious credence to his position and views, and even the staunchest opponents gladly hear him out. Awe can often turn the tide in a tense negotiation, tipping the scales from 'potentially\" to \"definitely.\" As with other Majesty powers, Awe ceases to be useful when more pressing matters come to the fore. Personal safety always takes precedence, and any commotion pulls even the most rapt listener from his reverie of attention. Those subjected to the character's stature likely remember how good they felt around him, and tailor future reactions accordingly.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "1 Scene",
    "successText": "When he wills it, all eyes fall on the vampire and delights in what he's doing. For the rest of the scene, he suffers no penalties to Social rolls from his actions or appearance — even if he's just beaten another man to death or waved a gun in a crowded nightclub. Given a chance, he can talk his way out of minor criminal offenses and almost any social faux pas. Those individuals affected by Awe raptly fawn over the user.\n\nAs the center of attention, he adds his Majesty dots as automatic successes to Social Confrontation: Compels, Attacks, or Commands when talking to people around him. This bonus only applies when using Social Confrontation, not to other abilities of Majesty.\n\nAs a whole, a character may not single out an individual subject in a crowd to Awe. He simply ' 'turns on the charm\" and lets it work its magic. However, with a word, he can call a single person in the room to his side not by any mystical compulsion, but by the simple animal attraction of being the focus of one so alluring and great. Anyone paying attention to him subtracts his Majesty dots from any Wits + Perception rolls to notice anything other than the vampire.",
    "sourcePage": 185
  },
  {
    "group": "Majesty",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Revelation",
    "flavor": "The allure and reassurance of a Vampire with this power is enough to make others forego caution and share their innermost feelings and secrets.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Majesty minus subject's Intelligence",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Majesty",
    "opposedBy": "subject's Intelligence",
    "actionText": "Instant. Victim can spend 1 Willpower to ignore the effects of this power until it is used again.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The vampire's manipulative efforts are obvious for the intended subject to see. No more of the vampire's attempts at Revelation on the same subject work for the remainder of the night.",
    "failureText": "No more of the vampire's attempts at Revelation on the same subject work for the remainder of the scene.",
    "successText": "The subject bares his soul. A few complimentary or compassionate words or a heartfelt look from the vampire can break down a person's prudence and fear, inspiring a desire to share deep feelings or dark secrets in an upwelling of affection or release.\n\nWhile a vampire can gain the information he seeks by use of this power, he must be prepared for a litany of confessions from a particularly guilty or tormented subject. A longtime criminal might have many sins to confess, for example, only one of which is important to the character using Revelation. A successful Manipulation + Intrigue roll may be required for the vampire to steer the confession to a matter of interest. If the roll fails, the vampire must endure everything that the subject has to say. Should the confession go on for more than an hour, the subject turns to particularly deep, dark or dangerous secrets. The subject remembers what he confessed after the effects of the power have passed. He undoubtedly can't explain his lack of discretion, and he might do anything in his power to compensate for his ' 'error in judgment.\"\n\nSuggested Modifiers",
    "notes": "Modifier Situation\n\n+3 The subject is a thrall to the Vampire, under a full Vinculum to her.\n\n+3 The subject is already under the influence of Entrancement.\n\n+2 The subject is already under the influence of Awe.\n\n+2 The subject is under the second stage of a partial Vinculum to the Vampire.\n\n+2 Power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has a blood tie\n\nThe subject is affected by the first stage of a partial Vinculum to the Vampire.\n\n-1 The subject is an enemy.\n\n-2 The Revelation user has inflicted violence upon the subject in the scene.",
    "sourcePage": 186
  },
  {
    "group": "Majesty",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "Entrancement",
    "flavor": "This power is perhaps the closest thing that Majesty gets to serious command over the thoughts of another. Its power warps the emotional state of the subject, making him a willing servant of the vampire. Subjects retain their sense of identity and free will, believing that every instinct to serve and admire comes of their own volition. When the duration of an Entrancement ends, however, confusion and displacement arise, often leading to mixed feelings. Similar to falling out of love, re-entrancing a former \"lover\" is difficult at best.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Empathy + Majesty minus subject's Wits",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Empathy",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Majesty",
    "opposedBy": "subject's Wits",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "A dramatic failure offends the subject, making him immune to the vampire's \"advances\" for the rest of the night.",
    "failureText": "The Entrancement fails and the subject reacts to the vampire violently.",
    "successText": "His victim is now thoroughly obsessed with the vampire for the next week or so, and he can play with her feelings and desires like they were a cheap harmonica. The vampire can ask one of his Entranced victims to do something in such a way that they feel an obsessive need to accomplish it. He could ask them to kill someone, to give him money, or to perform debased and degrading acts for his amusement. An Entranced victim will do whatever he wants within reason if the request would lead to the victim taking lethal damage or hitting a breaking point, the victim will save her own skin.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "An exceptional success indentures the target for at least a few months, and potentially a year or more. When the period of effect wears off, the subject does not necessarily bear the vampire any ill will. After all, he has no idea that something unnatural has taken place. He merely comes to the conclusion that he no longer feels the way he once did for the character (even if ' 'once\" was an hour ago). At this time, he likely returns to going about his own life, content with never seeing the vampire again. Typically, a vampire who wishes to retain the attention of an affected subject calls upon more reliable (or stringent) methods of indenturing, such as the Vinculum.\n\nSuggested Modifiers",
    "notes": "Modifier Situation\n\n+2 Power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has a blood tie\n\n-3 The Vampire has already successfully Entranced the subject within a week.",
    "sourcePage": 186
  },
  {
    "group": "Majesty",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "Summoning",
    "flavor": "This rather potent power allows the vampire to call any individual he knows personally to his side. In short, the vampire reaches out with the force of his personality and makes a colleague, acquaintance, rival or outright enemy know without doubt that he wishes that person to attend him at once.\n\nThere is no limit on the distance or range of this power, but summoning someone halfway around the world takes time, even under the best of circumstances. The subject of Summoning takes the most direct possible routes to the vampire's location and intuitively knows when the vampire moves, though doesn't necessarily know the final destination until he gets there (it's like sort of direction sense).\n\nThe summoned individual knows to whose presence he travels, and he may pause to make arrangements before departure, if necessary. In other words, the compulsion is strong and the subject doesn't dilly-dally unnecessarily, but he is hardly a single-minded drone in his desire to reach the vampire. The subject does, however, use any resources at his disposal to see that he arrives both safely and punctually.\n\nThe call of a summoning fades with the first rays of the sun at dawn. Therefore, if a subject is truly far away or a sufficiently fast means of transportation is not available to him, he can deny the compulsion to attend the summoner at dawn. Indeed, he could return home and go about his business.\n\nIf the subject is predisposed toward the summoner, however, he may continue the quest of his own accord. During the day, he is under his own power to find the vampire and loses his \"direction sense.\" Another vampire is not compelled to seek the summoner to the very light of day, causing his Final Death.\n\nThe seeker finds shelter beforehand. Unless the subject already knows where and how to reach the summoning vampire, the call must be repeated each night until the subject arrives. If, as in the preceding case with the resistant subject, the distance is too far or available transportation is too slow, the subject might never arrive. He falls into a cycle of being drawn to and fleeing from the summoner.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Persuasion + Majesty minus subject's Wits",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Majesty",
    "opposedBy": "subject's Wits",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The target is aware that the vampire tried to summon him, and is immune to that vampire's Summoning attempts for the rest of the story.",
    "failureText": "The character loses or ties the contested roll. The subject remains unaware of the Summoning, and nothing happens.",
    "successText": "The character wins the contested roll by getting the most successes. Complete success brings the subject as quickly as possible within the space of one night, without questioning the motivations or origins of the summons.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "The character wins the contested roll with five or more successes. An exceptional success draws the subject to the vampire with all deliberate haste, no matter how long the journey takes. The compulsion to arrive prevails even during daylight, and the subject's sense of the summoner's location persists throughout.\n\nSuggested Modifiers",
    "notes": "ROLL RESULTS\n\nModifier Situation\n\n+3 The subject is dedicated or loyal to the summoner.\n\n+2 Power is turned on a vampire with whom the user has blood tie.\n\nThe subject is predisposed toward or friendly with the summoner.\n\n+1 The summoner knows exactly where the subject is.\n\nThe summoner doesn't know specifically where the subject is, but knows that he is in the same domain.\n\n-1 The summoner has no idea where the subject is.\n\n-1 The subject resents being summoned or dislikes the summoner.\n\n-3 The summoner has no idea where the subject is and he's more than 500 miles away.\n\n-3 The subject has urgent business elsewhere or reviles the summoner.\n\n-5 The summoner has no idea where the subject is and he's on the other side of the world.",
    "sourcePage": 187
  },
  {
    "group": "Majesty",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Sovereignty",
    "flavor": "One of the most potent of all Vampire abilities, Sovereignty augments the power of a vampire's personal deportment to incredible levels. The power of this level of Majesty is a terrifying thing to behold, and a difficult thing to rein in once it's let loose. Elders are wise not to abuse it or use it too flippantly, as its power is most effective when used both sparingly and exactingly. Despite its mighty prowess, Sovereignty is a finely honed tool scalpel, rather than a sword.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Willpower + 3 Vitae per scene",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Sovereignty is considered ' 'always active\" during scenes in which it is invoked, though it may be turned off at the Vampire's discretion.",
    "successText": "ROLL RESULTS",
    "notes": "Success: The newly empowered mien inspires devotion, respect and fear in all those who stand in the vampire's proximity. You are seen as their Lord and Master, the King of their world and the Epitome of their ideals. The weak-willed (and cunning) supplicate themselves for the chance to serve, and the stout of heart find themselves at a loss to do anything but acquiesce. Sovereignty's power influences decisions, breaks hearts, cripples confidence and shakes the ambitious to the foundations of their determination. The vibe the vampire gives off makes others quick to surrender, and the thought of risking his displeasure quails even the heartiest souls. Raising one's voice to a Sovereign Vampire seems unthinkable; aggressing upon him seems impossi ble.\n\nVictims of this power must spend a point of Willpower and make roll of Wits + Resolve minus the Sovereign characters' dots in Majesty must be made every time someone wishes to make a new attack, use a Discipline in some way the Sovereign would disapprove of, or criticize the vampire who uses Sovereignty.\n\nThose under Blood Bond or ghouled by the Sovereign vampire have no option but to obey their superior. No amount of their Willpower of Resolve will allow them to anything their Sovereign considers untoward or disallowable. Although any vampires to which the Sovereign is the subject of a Blood Bond to can ignore the effects of this power by spending 1 Willpower, no roll is necessary. This power is not broken by any action the Sovereign vampire takes. No matter how violent or raging or abusive, the power remains in effect until the Sovereign chooses to relinquish it or the scene ends. Even in the Sovereign falls into Torpor, the power remains in effect until the end of the scene.",
    "sourcePage": 188
  },
  {
    "group": "Obfuscate",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Conceal",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Stealth + Obfuscate",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Stealth",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Obfuscate",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "One Scene",
    "dramaticFailureText": "Some element in the scene draws attention to the item being hidden, and it is immediately noticed.",
    "failureText": "The power has no effect.",
    "successText": "The character can hide a single object from sight, of about the size easily concealed in the palm of a hand to an item that can only barely be squeezed into a large jacket pocket. No one can see the item obviously held by your vampire. If someone actively searches for that specific item, they will not see it unless they know specifically what it is. Indeed, \"specific\" is very literal here — a character looking for \"an old book' does not find it, though she does if she's looking for \" the Ordo Dracul Kogaion's handwritten and leatherbound copy of 17th-century ritual\", and has seen it before, then she will find it.\n\nSuccesses Situation",
    "notes": "1 An item that can be concealed under a jacket without too obvious a bulge.\n\n2 An item too large to be naturally hidden, but still small enough to be carried easily.\n\n3 An object a searcher knows exactly what they seek.\n\nThis power allows the character to assume the image of whomever they want others to perceive them as. A ' 'frail old woman,\" or a ' 'young lothario,\" or as a specific person, like ' 'Jon's friend Jason.\" People perceive the vampire as though he were who they'd expect to see based on his chosen disguise. However, this perception is as those expect, not with exacting details as your vampire dictates. So if you are projecting as a \"frail old woman,\" each person may perceive a different frail old woman. If you are projecting \"Jon's friend Jason,\" then you are indeed Jason, but how you are dressed, the age, and any details of what they may look like may differ from persn to person who know Jason. And for those who don't know Jason, the visage could vary wildly with assumptions! Mirrors are of no use here, the vampire only sees themselves if they are reflected at all.\n\nDice Pool: Empathy + Obfuscate\n\nThe vampire appears in the mind's eye of observers as they expect them to. For all visual reasons, the vampire looks close to their intended subject. This is merely in the minds of all around, the vampire does not change form physically in any way.\n\nNOTE: for Nosferatu your ugliness still bleeds through, making the subject look worse for the wean ' or \"strung 0111'.\n\nThis discipline gives them no ability to act or behave or know what their desired target does. Any social interactions require you to roll a Subterfuge roll minus targets Resolve, to fool whomever they are interacting.\n\nFail: If you fail the roll, some aspect of your Beast bleeds into the visage of the person, giving everyone a Condition: Uneasy 3 to interact with you. A Botch forces them to act on this immediately with panic or anger.\n\nThe vampire may chose I specific aspect, visual detail of the subject they are\n\nThe vampire may hide anything that is affecting them, such as being wet,\n\nThe vampire may alter I item they are carrying of about the size of a briefcase, backpack, shotgun or smaller.",
    "sourcePage": 189
  },
  {
    "group": "Obfuscate",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Mind Blank",
    "flavor": "What good is being unseen if others can still hear your thoughts? Vampires with this Discipline can hide their mind and Aura from anyone and any power which may detect or affect it. Their thoughts cannot be read, their choices cannot be dominated, and their aura is utterly invisible to all.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "none",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Concentration each attempt, with rolls allowed for up to I Scene. Any time",
    "successText": "When successfully in effect( see Duration roll requirement above) , this vampire's mind and Aura are utterly unreadable and blank. And they are immune to the effects of any mind-altering or mind-affecting powers.",
    "notes": "Cost: 3 Vitae someone tries to use a power to affect or detect this vampire's mind, the Obfuscating\n\nAction: Instant vampire must make a Wits + Resolve roll (at\n\nDuration: 1 Scene if the attacker's Blood Potency is higher than yours). Success indicates you are utterly unaffected by that person and they may not try again that scene. Any new attacker must roll. This Discipline ends at the end of the scene.",
    "sourcePage": 190,
    "bonusOptions": [
      {
        "successCost": 1,
        "effect": "2 mimicking. 2 bloody. 3"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "group": "Obfuscate",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "Mask of a Thousand Faces",
    "flavor": "Objects and the Faces Mask of a Thousand Faces will give the look of the person you intend, and to a point, even their clothes and jewelry and other such personal affects. But only things typically associated with that person. Any items your vampire is holding, the visage also is holding, so if you are holding a shotgun, then everyone sees this.\n\nSo if you are impersonating a cop, you will appear to have a bullet resistant vest and a pistol. If you are a senator, you may have a suit and nice tie. But you don't actually have such items. You can appear to interact with them, the person may think you draw a gun or adjust your tie, but if they come into physical contact the Discipline automatically falls away and ends its effects for that person. Other people may get Perception rolls to shake the effects if they witness someone else's act of reve ation.\n\nAs well, any major effects to your vampire will be mimicked on the person you project as. If you are gunshot, covered in blood, or dripping wet from fall in the river, then that also his how the projected person ooks too!",
    "sourcePage": 190
  },
  {
    "group": "Obfuscate",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "Cloak the Gathering",
    "flavor": "more powerful manifestation of Conceal, Cloak the Gathering allows the vampire to extend her powers of Obfuscate to an area.",
    "vitaeCost": 4,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "4 Vitae + 1 Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "1 hour of walking the area, observing it Only 1 structure may be cloaked at a time. from every angle possible.",
    "duration": "Indefinite or until vampire chooses to remove cloak",
    "successText": "The vampire may use the Conceal power on an area roughly the size of a one-car gatage. Any non-digital attempts to see this location are simply ignored by the mind. People will walk around it, avoid bumping into it, and othetwise not interact with it — yet still will see nothing there (or will see a logical extension of some attached place so as to make this location not a place someone could be at all.) Anyone and anything within this area is hidden, even if it has windows or has light which emanates out. Any noises are unable to be heard. And any weather effects or environmental effects are seen to logically not affect this place at all.",
    "notes": "The area can be made as large as a typical single family home when you vampire has 5 dots in Blood Potency. And can be as large as an average city block at 8+ dots in Blood Potency.\n\nAny attempts to see this place can be made if the viewer makes a Perception + Investigation roll minus the vampire's dots in Obfuscate. However, this roll may only be made if there is a narrative reason to think there ought to be a structure here (typically this comes from having being forced to interact with it such as accidental car crash into it, or taking a photo of a building and then reviewing the photo only to see a structure that was not there before). Anyone using Auspex will automatically see this structure as per Clash of Wills.",
    "sourcePage": 191
  },
  {
    "group": "Obfuscate",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Vanish from the Mind's Eye",
    "flavor": "An essential expression of the Vampire's secretive nature, Cloak of Night allows a vampire to literally fade from sight, becoming completely invisible to observers. Though it's easiest to invoke the capability out of sight, Cloak of Night is so potent that it allows the vampire to vanish even under direct observation. The actual process of fading away is subtle, but its effect on witnesses might not be. Mortals are likely to panic and flee the area, or to find some way to justify what they've witnessed, even if it means constructing new memories of the event. (\"l glanced away for a moment, and when I looked back I think I saw him leave through that door.\" ) Some especially weak-willed mortals might simply forget the vampire was ever present at all. Vampires, of course, tend to be less strongly impacted, though even an experienced and knowledgeable vampire might find the experience somewhat startling.",
    "vitaeCost": 4,
    "costText": "4 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Intelligence + Obfuscate minus targets Perception",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Intelligence",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Obfuscate",
    "opposedBy": "targets Perception",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The character believes he has vanished from sight, when in fact he has not.",
    "failureText": "The character does not vanish at all, and knows it.",
    "successText": "The character fades from notice but does not vanish. He simply is not noticeable. Unless he draws attention to himself, observers will simply disregard him or glance over him. If someone is actively looking for the obfuscated character they gain are at a -4 Dice penalty to see them. Successful use of Vanish renders the character finvisible' until he does something to draw attention (launching an attack, smashing a window, shouting a warning), in which case the cloak drops. Hiding oneself with this power even accounts for a character inadvertently revealing himself - A vampire won't typically bump into bystanders, as they subconsciously move out of his way. No one hears when he steps on a squeaky floorboard unless they have somehow already pierced his Obfuscate.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "Five or more successes are rolled for the Discipline user. The character vanishes from plain sight. Witnesses reconstruct the scene in their memory to justify the character's disappearance.",
    "sourcePage": 191
  },
  {
    "group": "Protean",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Bestial Perceptions",
    "flavor": "The Beast was never human. It remembers no childhood, no first kiss, no whispered lie beneath the sheets. It was born knowing only hunger, rage, and the hunt. And in that hunt, its senses were always sharper, always crueler.\n\nWhen a vampire calls upon Bestial Perceptions, they do not merely sharpen their sight or hearing. They allow the Beast to rise closer to the surface, lending its cold, predatory awareness to every nerve ending. The world becomes a field of prey: every heartbeat a drum, every breath a signal, every footfall a map.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Academics + Protean",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Academics",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Protean",
    "actionText": "Instant; the vampire must take a moment of stillness, inhaling deeply, as the Beast awakens behind the eyes.",
    "failureText": "As Success, but the Beast gnaws at the edges of control. The vampire suffers a Compel -2 for the remainder of the scene tied to predatory obsession (chosen by S T). The world throbs with overwhelming signals that threaten to pull the vampire into hunt-state.",
    "successText": "The vampire gains one animalistic sense chosen at activation, active for 1 scene. This sense far exceeds human capability, granting 43 dice on any roll that could benefit from it. The vampire may use it reflexively and without thought, as though it were always theirs.",
    "sourcePage": 192,
    "bonusOptions": [
      {
