  {
    "group": "Protean",
    "level": 2,
    "name": "Claws of the Wild",
    "flavor": "Upon activation of this power, the vampire's nails grow into long, fearsome talons, capable of rending bone and flesh alike. This power makes many Gangrel truly fearsome combatants, and other Vampires tend to give them a wide berth. Note that not every vampire's claws look the same. Some seem to be modeled after real-world animals', such as wolves', tigers' or the talons of birds of prey, while other claws seem to have no natural correlative at all. As might be expected, when these last vampires unsheathe their claws, they tend to greatly unnerve those around them.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "This power involves no roll.",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "1 scene",
    "successText": "The wicked claws grow by silent act of will on the part of the vampire, and they can be sprouted from both a vampire's hands and feet. Imbued with the unholy power of Vitae, these ferocious weapons offer a +1 bonus to attack pools in unarmed close combat, and inflict lethal damage. Of course, these bonuses require that the character actually attacks with his claws. In addition to their combat potential, the claws add two dice to any climbing-related rolls as long as they remain invoked.\n\nThe claws remain for the scene unless the vampire retracts them prematurely. In addition a vampire may spend a willpower point and not only activate his claws but also distend his jaw and make his vampiric bite much more vicious. While the claws work as above, a vampire attack including his bite now does an additional bonus to attack pools and does Lethal damage.",
    "sourcePage": 193
  },
  {
    "group": "Protean",
    "level": 3,
    "name": "Feral Whispers",
    "flavor": "The Vampire's connection with his own feral nature now allows him to command them as well. He demands, and they obey as best they can. The vampire must make eye contact, and convey to an animal precisely what he wants it to do. The animal obeys to the best of its ability, but its nature and intelligence might cause it to interpret its orders in unusual ways. Once it has received an order, the animal carries it out, assuming it can be completed before the following sunrise. At dawn, the compulsion ceases, regardless of whether the task is complete or not. It is extremely difficult to order an animal to do anything blatantly against its nature (such as ordering a sparrow to attack someone) or obviously self-destructive (such as forcing a guard dog to lie down in front of a moving truck). A vampire can give single orders to only one animal at a time, but he may have as many animals currently under his control as circumstances allow, as long as he can give each one its orders individually.",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Protean (-6 dice if target is nota nocturnal predator)",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Protean",
    "actionText": "Instant Obedience also requires a contested roll if the animal is already under the control of another Vampire with this power (in which case Obedience successes achieved must exceed those rolled for the original vampire).",
    "dramaticFailureText": "Dramatic failure indicates that the animal either attacks the character or does the exact opposite of what he commands it to do. In addition, that animal is immune to any further use of Protean by that character until the next sunset.",
    "failureText": "Failure indicates that the character cannot command or communicate with the animal.",
    "successText": "Success indicates that the animal obeys the character's orders to the best of its abilities.",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "Exceptional success indicates that the animal develops a strong, if temporary, affection for the character. It attempts to please and to anticipate orders, even when not actively following a specific command.",
    "notes": "Convey Message Additionally to giving the animal a command, the vampire can give it a message to convey. It must be short and simple. The vampire imbues a message in a small animal who then carries the message to its intended recipient, relaying it in the vampire's own voice. The animal can be any creature, but if it must travel long distances, a bird is best. The vampire must have a clear image in his mind of who the animal must find. When the animal arrives at its destination, the message is delivered as the vampire spoke it, the illusion of his voice emanating from the animal. Once the power is used, the animal has a number of days equal to successes to find its designated recipient and deliver the message, otherwise the compulsion fades and the message is lost.\n\nCost: 3 Vitae",
    "sourcePage": 193
  },
  {
    "group": "Protean",
    "level": 4,
    "name": "Wild Form",
    "flavor": "The canon of vampire legend is filled with stories of undead lords taking the semblance of the \"children of the night.\" This power is the source of those legends. With it, a Vampire can take the shape of an ordinary animal, most commonly a bat ora wolf. While in this form, the vampire retains his mind and temperament, but he can call upon the physical qualities of his newfound body (increased speed and senses as a wolf, sonar sense and flight as a bat). With time and experience, the character can learn to adopt the other form (bat or wolf) or others.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae | Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "This power requires no roll to activate.",
    "successText": "While in animal form, a vampire can use any Discipline in his repertoire except for Blood Sorcery, Dominate (which becomes impossible when one tries to bark and yip commands), and other such Social Disciplines.\n\nWhen this power is first acquired, only bat or wolf form is available. With Storyteller permission, every 10 experience points spent allows character access to transform into a new form. A vampire can turn into a max number of forms equal to his Blood Potency. A vampire in animal form is not subject to Feral Whispers, or Ride the Wild Spirit. Dominate may still be used on him in whatever form — human or animal — he assumes.\n\nEach animal form conveys a separate host of benefits, here are some suggested examples: As a wolf, the vampire's claws and teeth inflict lethal damage and add a +1 bonus to attack pools, running Speed is doubled, and two bonus dice are added to any Wits + Resolve rolls to be aware of events that happen in the wolf's environment. As a bat, the vampire's Strength becomes I, but he can fly at a Speed of 20 (plus five more per dot of Celerity activated); three dice are added to all hearing-based rolls, and Defense increases by two. Other options: Coyote, Fox, Cat (house, bob, lynx, etc), Owl, Scorpion, Alligator, Mountain Lion.",
    "notes": "The transformation from human to animal or back again requires just a few seconds. No clothing or possessions shift with the vampire. He can remain in his animal state until he chooses to shift back. A vampire can even sleep the day away in animal form, but must still avoid sunlight, which always affects vampires regardless of form. In addition, sleeping in animal form is taxing on the vampiric state, requiring the expenditure of I additional Vitae upon awakening. A vampire, however, my not feed and gain Vitae in animal form.\n\nWhile in Wild Form, the character may activate Claws of the Wild. As with normal uses of that power, these claws are obviously supernatural or otherwise remarkable, and even those unfamiliar with the world of the Damned will know that something is not quite right about the animal in question.",
    "sourcePage": 194
  },
  {
    "group": "Protean",
    "level": 5,
    "name": "Ride the Wild Sprit",
    "flavor": "By locking eyes with an animal, a vampire may psychically enter the creature's body and possess it as though it were his own. Some Vampires believe that doing so actually transfers the vampire's soul into the beast, though other, less mystically minded Vampires disagree. Regardless, the animal's own mind and instincts are completely subsumed, allowing the Vampire free reign to take whatever actions he chooses in the creature's body. The vampire's own body falls into a torpor-like state and appears for all intents and purposes to be a corpse. Until the character returns, his body cannot be awakened by any means (though Vampire urban legends tell of ghosts possessing such bodies and wreaking havoc). Also, it is whispered among certain circles that some vampires, addicted to the sensations of life they experience while riding an animal, remain too long in that form and forget their true nature.",
    "vitaeCost": 4,
    "costText": "4 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Protean (-5 dice if target is not a nocturnal predator)",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolDiscipline": "Protean",
    "actionText": "Contested",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The character fails to bond with the animal; the animal grows hostile and is immune to any further uses of Protean by that character until the next sunset.",
    "failureText": "The character loses or ties the contested action and fails to bond with the animal.",
    "successText": "The character wins the contested action and occupies the animal's body. He can use Protean but no other Disciplines while doing so•",
    "exceptionalSuccessText": "The character wins the contested action with five or more successes and occupies the animal's body, and can also use Auspex and Majesty while doing so. If the roll made for the character wins the contested roll and gets successes in excess of the animal's Resolve dots, the character is in total control and his mind remains clear. If his roll succeeds but garners number of successes equal to or lower than the animal's Resolve dots, a Willpower point must be spent for the character to take any actions contrary to the animal's instincts. Otherwise, bestial urges and impulses cloud the vampire's mind.\n\nAdditionally, if the player wins the contested roll but gets a number of successes equal to or lower than the animal's Resolve, his character's consciousness is so closely intertwined with the beast's that he maintains some bestial behaviors even after returning to his own form. Until the player spends a total of three Willpower points specifically to overcome this effect the points can't, say, be spent to gain three extra dice in unrelated rolls the character continues to think and feel in an animalistic manner. (This effect has no \"hard\" mechanical applications, but it should be roleplayed. If the character doesn't indulge in animalistic activity, the Storyteller should feel free to either dock the character future experience points or spend Willpower automatically for the character when a particularly bestial response is appropriate but not displayed.)\n\nWhile possessing an animal, a character can travel as far from his own body as he wishes and is unharmed by daylight, but he must still force himself to stay awake during the day. The vampire may choose to end the possession and return to his body at any time, regardless of distance. This occurs automatically if the vampire fails to remain awake.\n\nAny injuries inflicted on the animal also affect the vampire's body. If the animal dies while the vampire is still present, the Vampire falls into torpor immediately. (Some believe that the soul attempts to find its way back to its own body during this time.) If the Vampire's physical form is destroyed, his psyche remains trapped in the animal until he finally fails to remain awake, at which point his spirit is lost to oblivion and is unrecoverable. It is possible, though uncommon, for a vampire to neglect his physical body long enough for it to starve into torpor while he's ' 'out.\" If a vampire's dormant body slips or is forced into torpor, the vampire's spirit automatically returns to its body.\n\nSuggested Modifiers Modifier Situation",
    "notes": "The animal is a predatory mammal (wolf, cat, insectivorous bat).\n\nThe character has already successfully used Feral Whispers on the animal he now attempts to command with Obedience.\n\nThe animal is another sort of mammal, a predatory bird or a large reptile (rat, owl, and alligator).\n\n-1 The animal is another sort of bird or a small reptile (pigeon, snake).\n\n-1 The order is contradictory to the animal's nature or set of inherent abilities.\n\n-1 The animal is a ghoul.\n\n-3 The animal is an insect or fish.\n\n-5 The order is blatantly suicidal.",
    "sourcePage": 195
  },
  {
    "group": "Tartarus",
    "level": 1,
    "name": "Shoulders of Atlas",
    "flavor": "They say the Beast knows only hunger. But it knows burden too. It knows the strain of eternity pressing down upon flesh that no longer lives, upon bones hollowed by death but made monstrous by Vitae. There are Kindred who embrace this burden, who call upon the oldest strength—the primal force that crushed mountains and dragged the world through the void before the gods themselves could speak. These vampires do not lift things. They conquer them. Steel groans in their hands. Stone whimpers as they shoulder its weight. And for every ounce hoisted, the Beast purrs, feeding not on blood, but on dominance over gravity itself. Atlas bore the heavens. The Kindred bear the world entire, if only for a moment.",
