    "castingDuration": "2 seconds",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The lich is wracked with visions of their own death, a thousand different ways. And they gain a Stain.",
    "failureText": "Well that was and awkward hug. Nothing happens.",
    "successText": "Each minute the lich touches the victim they age 10 years. They can age a person for as many minutes as the lich has in Blood Potency. The Storyteller can then decide what sort of awful effects this can have.",
    "sourcePage": 208
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Blood of the Earth",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "The Tzimisce with this ritual has converted all of her blood into dark viscous substance far more deadly than any regular poison.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "Once activated, all of the vampire's blood and all blood they consume is affected.",
    "duration": "I Scene",
    "castingDuration": "I second per point of Vitae in their system",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The lich's blood becomes as earth, dust. They lose a number of Vitae as it becomes dust equal to their dots in Stamina.",
    "failureText": "Aunt Jemima or Log Cabin? Nothing happens.",
    "successText": "The Tzimisce's blood becomes dark and oily. This gooey blood clings to sulfaces and is hard to remove; to do so in a hurry requires concerted effort and often chemicals or tools of some sort. Because of its viscosity, feeding from a lich with Blood of the Earth only gains 1 Vitae if a successful Resolve Stamina roll is made (assuming you got your fangs into the lich in the first place). As well the blood refuses to burn, giving the lich he ability to soak Fire damage equal to their dots in Stamina. (this stacks with Fortitude soak). Hospitality is the highest, and most dangerous, peak in ???, where the biting winds and frigid temperatures will kill an unprotected man in mere moments. A pelfect setting to destroy one's enemies.",
    "notes": "Cost: 2 Vitae\n\nDice Pool: Dexterity + Streetwise\n\nAction: The Tzimisce must toss the requisite blood into the air\n\nCasting Time: 2 seconds\n\nDramatic Failure: The Tzimisce is forced to grant perfect hospitality and social grace to all present in the scene. And may not act violently against anyone.\n\nFailure: Why do you have the thermostat turned so high? Nothing happens.\n\nSuccess: Frigid wind arises all around the Tzimisce for a number of seconds equal to successes rolled. This wind causes one level of Bashing damage each turn and numbs the bodies of anyone except the Tzimisce caught within, reducing their movement by half and Dexterity pools by two. On the turn after this power expires, every victim of this power must roll Stamina + Resolve or, if alive take 1 point of Lethal frostbite damage, if dead freeze solid for 1 minute per dot in Blood Potency the lich has. A useful side effect of this power is that the Tzimisce is immune to the cold and force of wind while this is active; when trekking in mountains or other environs where wind may be a hazard, they need only activate ???? Hospitality to ward themselves from danger.",
    "sourcePage": 209
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "The Banks of the ???",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "When the rains come in the ???, the ??? leaps its banks and whole villages are swept away, along with any person, living or dead, unwise enough not to seek higher ground. The Tzimisce using this ritual carries with her the surging might of the ??? wherever she goes.",
    "dicePoolText": "Strength + Brawl",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Strength",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Brawl",
    "actionText": "The Tzimisce releases her blood into running water - a river or stream is most common, though a rivulet or even just runoff from thawing snow is enough",
    "duration": "These rushing waters last for a number of minutes equal to the lich's Blood Potency, or twice that number if the source of the flooding is any ???? river.",
    "castingDuration": "2 Seconds",
    "successText": "The blood bolsters the flow, causing it to swell and surge forward, a flash flood given horrific life. Anyone, as well as most anything not secured to the ground, caught in its path will be swept away towards the point of lowest elevation at a rate of a 30 yards a second (about 60mph), and must soak 5 dice of bashing damage per turn. Any subject somehow prevented from being carried downstream wedged against a wall or other immoveable object, for instance takes twice the normal amount of damage. Additionally, mortals must make a swimming roll each turn to avoid swallowing great mouthfuls of water; failure inflicts one additional level of Lethal drowning damage which cannot be soaked. Viscus Ventrue ghoul tells of a battlefield he once encountered deep within the ????. There, soldiers from both sides lay dead, their flesh stripped cleanly from their glistening bodies. More disturbing were the strange flesh-colored sacs that hung pregnant from tree branches above, some pulsing with inner life. He slashed at one within easy reach of his sword and what fell out was the most hideous sort of monstrosity, some half-formed melding of man and beast writhing in a pool of ichor and goo. Its soft, painful mewling brought about a seething response from the other cocoons.\n\nHe left before seeing what else might emerge.\n\nViscus is the signature occult knowledge the Tzimisce. It allows the magi to shape and sculpt their own or others flesh and bone. Magi who use Vicissitude create everything from useful body-tools to. expressive ornamentations. In order to use this power, the user must be able to work undisturbed on the subject, who thus either needs to be willing or restrained.",
    "notes": "NOTE: Viscus cannot overcome a Nosferatu's curse, no amount of altering their form will make them \"lovely\" or pleasant to look upon.",
    "sourcePage": 210
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Malleable Visage",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Viscus",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "actionText": "about 1 hour",
    "duration": "One scene",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The magi ruins their alabaster face and does one point of lethal damage to themselves that in no way may be soaked, blood must be spent to repair the damage",
    "failureText": "Just massaging my temples... nothing happens",
    "successText": "The vampire may alter their physical form, from height and build to voice and skin tone. All alterations are only cosmetic. And the magi must be able to physically mold the changes with their hands, using representative items to assist them (i.e. dirt to colour their skin darker, or sticks to add crow's feet to their eyes, and so on). All changes are cosmetic and essentially minor, no more than an inch may be lost or gained in height and overall build. More creative uses of this power, such as creating a secret pouch somewhere on the body or moving eyes around, are beyond the scope of this ritual",
    "notes": "Dramatic Success: The vampire may change their gender convincingly (though again only cosmetic) and gains a +2 bonus on any attempts to appear as some other recognizable person.",
    "sourcePage": 211
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Altered Form",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Intelligence + Viscus",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Intelligence",
    "actionText": "A few hours",
    "duration": "One Scene (or until the next day's sun rises if he also spends a point of Willpower)",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The magi ruins their cadaverous form and does one point of lethal damage per botch die to themselves that in no way may be soaked, blood must be spent to repair the damage",
    "failureText": "Hey why are you pointing at me?",
    "successText": "This ritual is similar to Malleable Visage, but allows the vampire to perform drastic and often grotesque alterations. Tzimisce often use this power to transform their servitors into monstrous guards, or themselves to more fully disguise or alter themselves based on their whims. Only crude yank-and-tuck alterations can be performed. A vampire may use this power to move clumps of skin, fat and muscle tissue, thus providing additional padding or strength where needed. For each success the vampire may shift the target's Physical Attributes around to represent the alterations. The end number of dots in any given stat have no maximums, but they must at a minimum of l.",
    "sourcePage": 211
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Bonecraft",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Strength + Viscus",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Strength",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "One Scene (or until the next day's sun rises if he also spends a point of Willpower)",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The magi ruins their cadaverous form and does one point of Aggravated damage per botch die to themselves that in no way may be soaked, blood and 1 Willpower must be spent to repair the damage",
    "failureText": "Some very awkward groping or deep tissue massage.",
    "successText": "This ritual allows a magi to manipulate bone in the same manner that flesh is shaped. Each success gained on the roll allows the magi to alter the form of any bone they grasp and shape it as they see fit. Up to 3 feet in size or shape may be altered adding spikes, spines, plates, hooks, vestigial limbs, distended limbs or jaws and so on.",
    "sourcePage": 211
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Horrid Form",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "vitaeCost": 5,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "5 Vitae + 1 point of Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "3 Seconds",
    "duration": "One Scene (or until the next day's sun rises if he also spends another point of Willpower)",
    "successText": "The Tzimisce use this power to become strange creatures. This form adds the following benefits: Their face warps into something out of a nightmare The magi's stature increases to a full eight feet Their skin becomes sickly greenish-grey or grayish black chitin offering I Fortitude above any they have (allowing for more than 5 Fortitude) All Physical Attributes increase by 3 dots (note that while the horrid vampire may have a stat now above 5, it will not convert to Potence, Celerity or Fortitude - Vitae must still be spent to gain those. Additionally, you may still only raise Potence, Celerity and Fortitude to 5 dots maximum.) Manipulation and Charisma drop to O dots except for intimidation Persuasion rolls in which the magi's normal dice pool increased by 3 Their arms become apelike and ropy, tipped with ragged black claws. All of the vampire's unarmed physical attacks do Lethal damage and gain +2 dice.",
    "sourcePage": 212
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Sanguis Genus",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "This blood Sorcery is about them being able to pull traits from people and modify their form based on the traits of those people. It could be skills or physical traits (how they look and sound). Or maybe, and maybe, this could be about how they make a mortal's skills or looks can be changed which will even apply as per science to their offspring.",
    "sourcePage": 212
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Brutal Ecstasy",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "Your character has mastered her senses to the point where she can reinterpret harmful sensation as empowering and ecstatic.",
    "notes": "System: This power does not require activation. Any time your character suffers a wound penalty, reverse it. The penalty dice become bonus dice instead. This power does not help your character act when Incapacitated. However, any time your character takes damage in her Incapacitated health level, she regains a spent point of Willpower.",
    "sourcePage": 212
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Adaptation",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "(note, mix with deflection of wooden doom as per below) Your character's body can shift in subtle, rapid ways to deflect the irritations and threats of her immediate environment. She can adapt her senses to make due with differences and absences.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae per hour of protection",
    "dicePoolText": "none",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Variable Once the Vitae has been spent for the remainder of the scene any item made of wood that would come in contact with the vampire's heart is rotted is disintegrated before any harm can be done to the heart.",
    "successText": "If the vampire spends 1 point of Willpower this power extends to all non-supernatural substances used to pierce their heart.",
    "notes": "System: Spend a blood point to activate Adaptation. The transformation takes one turn to complete. Aside from sunlight and fire, she suffers no harm from her environment, nor does she suffer environmental penalties. This includes damage and penalties from the weather, extreme temperatures, falls, and natural disasters.\n\nDeflection of Wooden Doom",
    "sourcePage": 212
  },
  {
    "group": "Vishap",
    "name": "Homunculus",
    "category": "Maleficence",
    "flavor": "Arguably the most ghoulish of Vicissitude's gifts, The Body Impolitic allows the user to invest a portion of her sentience in the flesh of her body, then sever it from the trunk to do the Tzimisce's bidding. System: By infusing an extremity with blood, the Tzimisce gives it awareness and a life of its own. Only one blood point is required, though some body parts can be gifted with more; the smallest of extremities (eyes, tongue, etc.) can hold only one, but a limb may hold two or three depending on where it is severed, the head can hold three, and the trunk can hold as much as five. Once imbued with awareness, the Tzimisce can then remove the extremity (Dexterity + Body Crafts to do so without inflicting one level of lethal damage upon herself) and set it free to do her bidding. When needed, an extremity can tear itself free from the main body; doing so requires two rounds and deals the usual level of lethal damage to the body. The Tzimisce can have as many servitor \"parts\" as she has extremities to use (and blood points to fuel them). While a body part is detached and still sentient, the Tzimisce cannot regrow it. Disembodied parts are always aware of all other parts of itself, but otherwise have only the sensory input normally available to them. Some Tzimisce will use Vicissitude to craft additional sensory organs onto these extremities to give them the tools to perform specific tasks (mouths and tongues to deliver messages, eyes to spy with, and so on). The master can use Disciplines through a disembodied extremity, but only if that body part can fulfill the Discipline's requirements (that is, it must have blood enough to fuel the Discipline, must be able to make eye contact if that's a requirement of the Discipline, etc.) If an extremity uses up its blood points to fuel a Discipline, it withers into nothingness moments after doing so. Independent extremities have one health level each. They are almost preternaturally aware of danger and gain three dots in Athletics. If an extremity is destroyed, it can be recreated by spending one blood point and successfully using Transmogrify the Mortal Clay and Rend the Osseous Frame to shape a replacement. So long as the Tzimisce has a living extremity tucked away somewhere, she can't fully be killed and may be able to reconstruct herself given time.",
    "sourcePage": 212
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Evil Eye",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "Some idea of karmic balance, where they can \"pay their nightly debt\" by tricking others into taking on the debt. This allows them to curse someone with their own doom they rolled for that night's waking.",
    "sourcePage": 214
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Enchanted Slumber",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "dicePoolText": "Intelligence + Subterfuge, difficulty Willpower The vampire may put a victim to sleep, and name an event that must occur that will enable the victim to awaken.",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Intelligence",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Subterfuge",
    "successText": "suc. one night. suc. one week. suc. one month. suc. one year.",
    "notes": "If the target is vampire, a willpower point must also be spent. If the event does not occur, the victim will stay asleep for up to the following time periods: suc. one turn.",
    "sourcePage": 214
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Master of Dreams",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "note: we have enter dreams to kill someone in Nightmare, so this is about literal dreamweaving and more",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Dreaming, difficulty 7 Resist: Manipulation + Dreaming, difficulty 7 The vampire may start changing the target's dreams, once she has entered them by using Dreamscape.",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "successText": "If either character dies in this dream, the character also dies for real.",
    "notes": "If the target is a vampire, a willpower point must also be spent. Once this power has been used, the target may also change her dreams with resistance rolls, and both characters may act in the dream as if they were awake.",
    "sourcePage": 214
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Shagun",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "The Ravnos recognize that many locales bear the mark of shagun. They're places of chaotic ill-omen. Using this ritual, a Ravnos promotes chaos by increasing the supernatural ambience of a place. The magi leaves a tiny smear of Vitae somewhere on or near the location, for example on a rock, a piece of furniture or a wall. Then the room goes cold. If outside, it starts to drizzle filthy, foul-smelling rain. Witch-fire plays around the trees. Apparitions flicker in and out of vision in the corner of one's eye. Rot and mold spontaneously appear. Urban decay appears briefly to accelerate. All Wits dice pools are penalized by -2 (other than for the magi). This effect lasts for a scene.\n\nThis curse is more subtle than the level three power, and affects much wider area. With this curse, the Baaliaffects an entire city or province with a feeling of gloom, despair and malaise. The more successes rolled on the Baali's Intelligence + Occult against difficulty of 9, the greater the effect. Crime and violence soar,petty angers become hatreds, the economy takes a downward spiral, marriages break up over trivial causes, and the would organizations become more fractious and less efficient - thus, this power is ideal for undermining Camarilla power structures in a city. A successful use of Auspex 9 or above will enable the user to determine that there is a molevolent outside aura on the region affected; othewise, the populace will simply assume that the times, they are a-changin' - for the worse.",
    "sourcePage": 214
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Abhishaap",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "The Ravnos who invokes voices of condemnation and takes on the role of patient pursuer. She lays a curse upon her victim, smearing blood on a small effigy. This done, whichever way the victim flees, he runs straight into the arms of the Ravnos. He leaves a room, and finds himself running back in. He turns a corner, and the next thing he knows, he's turned 180 degrees and he's traveling right back down the street, right back to where the smiling vampire awaits.\n\nIf the caster rolls the most successes, the next time the victim tries to run away from her, he runs in her direction. She need not move, only wait for him to arrive. The power lasts for a scene.\n\nChimeristry (Discipline of Mind) The Ravnos are heir to a legacy of illusion. Chimeristry is an art of conjuration; the vampire may draw upon images in her own mind to bring phantoms to life. These false images can confound mortal senses and supernatural creatures alike. The Ravnos are fond of using this power to seduce, swindle or enslave mortals, effectively purchasing their victims souls for a sack of bouillon that isn't there. Illusions created by Chimeristry work in the same way against supernatural sense such as Auspex. They may also be seen for what they are by a victim who \"proves\" the illusion's falsehood (e.g. a person who walks up to an illusory was, expresses his disbelief in it, and puts his hand through it effectively banishes the illusion).",
    "sourcePage": 215
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Ignis Fatuus",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "The vampire may conjure a minor, static mirage that confounds one sense, For instance, he may invoke a sulfurous stench, the image of a curtain, or the feel of raw silk. Note that although tactile illusions can be felt, they have no real substance; an invisible but tactile wall cannot confine anyone, and invisible razor-wire causes no real damage.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "N/A",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Till the caster leaves the scene or the caster ends it, no effort is required to do so Anyone interacting with the illusion may dispel its effect on their mind by interacting with it in such a way that causes them to realize it is false in some way.",
    "sourcePage": 215
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Fata Morgana",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "The vampire can now create illusions that appeal to all the senses, although they remain static. For example, the vampire could throw a mirage over a dank basement, making it appear to be a sumptuous boudoir, although she could not create flickering candles or a flowing fountain. Again, the dweomer has no solid presence, although it's easy enough to make a filthy mattress on two saw horses fell like a four-poster bed.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "N/A",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Till the caster leaves the scene or the caster ends it, no effort is required to do so Anyone interacting with the illusion may dispel its effect on their mind by interacting with it in such a way that causes them to realize it is false in some way.",
    "sourcePage": 215
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Apparition",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "N/A If Fata Morgana has been activated then the Ravnos may spend the Vitae to enhance their illusion.",
    "successText": "Apparition allows a vampire to give motion to an illusion created. Thus, the Ravnos could create the illusion of a living being, running water, fluttering drapes or a roaring fire. The creator spends their required cost to activate Chimeristry and as long as she concentrates, she can manipulate the illusory scene's movement from how she initially created it as she sees fit.",
    "sourcePage": 215
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Permanency",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "N/A If Fata Morgana has been activated then the Ravnos may spend the Willpower to enhance their illusion.",
    "successText": "This power allows a mirage to persist even when the vampire cannot see it. In this way, Ravnos often cloak their temporary havens in false trappings of luxury, or ward off trespassers with illusory guard dogs. This only costs an additional blood point to the normal cost to activate Chimeristry.",
    "sourcePage": 215
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "Horrid Reality",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "Rather than create simple illusions, the vampire can now project hallucinations so strong, the target believes completely that the images are real; a hallucinatory fore can burn him, an imaginary noose can strangle him, and an illusory wall can block him. This power affects only one target per dot in Blood Potency the vampire has, those not being affected can try to convince the victim that his terrors are not real, though he won't believe them.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation + Subterfuge + Chimeristry minus target's Perception",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Subterfuge",
    "opposedBy": "target's Perception",
    "actionText": "Instant",
    "duration": "Till the caster leaves the scene or the caster ends it, no effort is required to do so Each success on the roll can inflict one level of lethal damage on the victim; though this is not required.",
    "successText": "Likewise, this power need not cause harm it can also; assuming the vampires roll was successful over their targets roll, enable them to wholly convince the target the illusion is real, in touch and sensory and interaction. The targets mind fills in any gaps the illusion may require, and thus they may find themselves trapped in an illus01Y cage or dining on illusory food and tasting every savory bite.\n\nThe Way of Sorrow From Kruchina, a goddess of mourning depicted as a perpetually weeping woman, to Likho Odnoglazoye, the emaciated one-eyed hag who represented privation and suffering, the pantheons of Eastern Europe brim with deities overseeing starvation, misery, misfortune, bitterness and death. Whether the gods hear their names or the spirits merely attend their mention, a Ravnos who understands the obscure Way of Sorrow can invoke the most dismal powers of the divine. This way pays no heed to gods of revelry or plenty, only those whose attentions promise tragedy.",
    "notes": "If this power inflicts a number of levels of damage equal to the targets size, they suffer a heart attack and may die if they were susceptible to such, or if the target was a vampire, they go into Torpor regardless of Vitae stored. Otherwise this power cannot actually kill its victims. If the victim botches their defense roll they will likely gain a major derangement at the horrid memory of the pain or action suffered.\n\nfinal note: Dealing with forgotten gods requires propriety, especially those who govern such bleak concerns. If invoked incorrectly, the Way of Sorrow turns on the Ravnos. On a botch, the vampire suffers the effects of his own power as if she had scored five successes.",
    "sourcePage": 216
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "The Frustrations of Nestrecha",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "Named for the goddess of grief and failure, this power allows the Ravnos to rob an opponent of his resolve. The magi's stare saps the target's will to struggle. Although the victim is overcome with resigned pessimism or feelings of defeat, he can still take action to resist the sorcery, including combat, but only in a half-hearted or fearful way. He musters none of his usual passion or determination.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Persuasion + Sorrow minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Instant. The Ravnos must make eye contact with her victim.",
    "duration": "One minute per success",
    "successText": "Target may not spend Willpower points. In stowtelling terms, the victim might also lack strong moti vations or convictions for the power's duration (\"What difference does it make?\" or just don't care anymore\").",
    "sourcePage": 216
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "The Insults of Krivda",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "Any Ravnos worthy of the name can spit out a telling insult. But with this power, Krivda — a goddess of hatred and bitterness — ensures that the remark offends, enraging the recipient. This is a dangerous power to use, but it can unbalance a physically weak opponent who has access to powerful Disciplines or could be used to embarrass a vampire by causing him to suffer a violent outburst of the Beast vying for Dominion in public.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation Persuasion + Sorrow minus target's Intelligence",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "opposedBy": "target's Intelligence",
    "actionText": "Instant. The Ravnos insults the target in the most offensive and humiliating way she can conceive.",
    "duration": "Instant",
    "dramaticFailureText": "???",
    "successText": "The target flies into an uncontrollable rage. If the target is a vampire, he must immediately roll to resist Dominion.",
    "sourcePage": 216
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "The Weeping of Kruchina",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "The glare of a Ravnos can make someone so miserable that they do nothing but cry. This power does more than spill a few tears it causes hysterical bawling, wailing and gnashing of teeth. Some depressing notion overcomes the victim. Vampires might mourn their lost humanities or the passing of lovers who died long ago. Sometimes the source is more nebulous— Ravnos believe that it imparts the collected sorrow of their travels.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Persuasion + Sorrow minus target's Empathy",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Persuasion",
    "opposedBy": "target's Empathy",
    "actionText": "Instant. The Ravnos must sing to the target, a brief single chorus.",
    "duration": "1 hour per success",
    "successText": "The target is overwhelmed by intense misery and cries uncontrollably. Actions that require concentration are impossible for the power's duration. Vampires lose a Vitae point each hour as copious amounts of vitae stream from their eyes. This one-hour ritual involves the ingestion of crocodile blood. The vampire may whisper a magic word in a victim's ear, which will cause the victim to cry blood until the vampire removes her gaze from her, at a rate of one blood point per five minutes. The effects of this are slightly swollen capillaries around the eyes, and the usual effects of blood loss. (or maybe not cast gaze, and maybe causes memories of dark deeds they have done, worse if they murdered someone)",
    "sourcePage": 217
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "The Misfortune of Chernogolov",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "With a declaration that a person is doomed or destined to fail, the Ravnos summons the attention of Chemogolov—the silver-mustached god of misfortune to her victim. Under Chemogolov's unlucky gaze, he is hindered in everything he does. If he fails, he does so spectacularly.\n\nCondemnation The Children of Baal relish the opportunity to importune dark energies into the waking world. With this power, the Baali levies a curse upon her victim, drawing upon the tormented might of their blood to work their will upon fate.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "costText": "3 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Resolve + Empathy + Sorrow minus target's Resolve",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Resolve",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Empathy",
    "opposedBy": "target's Resolve",
    "actionText": "Instant. The Ravnos must call aloud for the misery upon their target such that their target can hear them.",
    "duration": "1 hour per success",
    "dramaticFailureText": "???",
    "successText": "The target automatically loses two successes on every roll he attempts. Botches experienced under the effects of the Misfortune of Chemogolov should be especially disastrous.",
    "notes": "System: An Intelligence + Occult roll (difficulty equal to the subject's Willpower) dictates the length and severity of the curse. Successes must be split between both these effects, as per the sidebar below. Curses with zero successes allotted to duration last for one night. The Baali may choose to end the curse at any time, but they rarely do so. Storytellers should feel free to invent creative or story-appropriate curses. At five successes, a Baali may rip a demon out of a mortal host or relic and fling it back to Hell, but they only do so against the most recalcitrant. Successes Duration Example success Up to one week \"Your lightest footfalls are as childish stomps.\" — All Steath rolls are at +3 difficulty. successes One month \"Sicken and wither, and feel the weakness of your blood.\" — The difficulty of all Stamina rolls increases by two, or vampire loses four soak dice. successes One year ' 'Live forever in fear: those you respect seek your betrayal.\" — The vampire suffers increased Social roll difficulties, or the character cannot benefit from Allies or Contacts. successes Ten years ' 'Feel the godling in your veins crushed, and your future progeny vanished.\" The character cannot Embrace childer or create ghouls. successes Permanent \"Doom will be upon you, and your missteps become fatal and final.\" Simple failures are considered botches.",
    "sourcePage": 217
  },
  {
    "group": "Mayaparisatya",
    "name": "The Starvation of Marena",
    "category": "Somnum",
    "flavor": "By invoking the wasted wife, the Ravnos summons the cold and starvation that is the domain of Marena. A frosty gale blasts the victim and leaves him emaciated as if he had just survived the coldest of winters. The frostbitten and starving victim clings to (un)life. The cold symbolizes of the passage of time in harsh conditions — healthy prey was scarce in the Eastern European winter",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Strength + Streetwise + Sorrow minus target's Stamina",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Strength",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Streetwise",
    "opposedBy": "target's Stamina",
    "actionText": "Instant. The Ravnos speaks the names of those left out in the cold by their target's actions.",
    "duration": "1 hour per success",
    "successText": "For each success, the victim takes two points of frosty Bashing damage that can be soaked normally. In addition to this damage, vampires lose one Vitae point for each of the Ravnos' successes as their blood freezes in their veins and shatters. Lasombra lose 2 per success for their bitter cold bites even more harshly at them and their murders.",
    "sourcePage": 218
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Devil's Touch",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "Magi use this ritual to place curses upon mortals who earn their ire. Using this ritual marks an individual invisibly, causing all those who come in contact with him to react to a mortal victim with hostility.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "Charisma + Subterfuge",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Charisma",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Subterfuge",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "I Night, until sun rises",
    "castingDuration": "Placement if a penny on the victim by magi personally (in a pocket, shoe, etc.)",
    "dramaticFailureText": "All of the magi's self-doubt and guilt, past crimes and failures come rushing back in a maddening array of voices and accusation. In addition to any Flaws, the magi loses a temporary point of Willpower.",
    "failureText": "none",
    "successText": "The mortal is treated as the most loathsome individual conceivable, and all who deal with him do everything in their power to make him miserable. Bums spit at an afflicted indi vidual, and children taunt him and barrage him with vulgarities. The things they shout are half-truths. They are dark reflections or worst aspects of the victim. While no secrets are told, there is a false perception that all people who meet the victim, hate them, look down upon them, or otherwise are angered by them for these aspects of self.",
    "sourcePage": 219
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Mourning Life Curse",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "The magi focuses the weight of purgatory on her victim, leaving him forever scarred and tainted from the overwhelming exposure. This strips down his fundamental identity and leaves him impure and weak.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "2 Vitae + 1 Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "Manipulation -k Empathy",
    "dicePoolAttribute": "Manipulation",
    "dicePoolSkill": "Empathy",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "I Scene",
    "castingDuration": "The magi must speak the dark truths of the void to her victim. As she opens her mouth, out comes the language of Hell. It is indecipherable by mundane methods, and even the magi herself does not truly know of what she speaks.",
    "dramaticFailureText": "The magi speaks the words of darkness and malice, the knowledge of which strips sanity from their minds, they Burn a dot of Intelligence.",
    "failureText": "none",
    "successText": "The victim is emotionally and mentally taken to a hell designed to torment them perfectly. What crushes the victim is not the pain or suffering. It is the immense and unavoidable sense of guilt — regardless of how callus they have become. This leaves him particularly vulnerable to the magi's dark temptations. The victim must immediately make a Humanity (or Morality) roll at their current value. As well the magi gains a Compel of Guilt over their target equal to successes rolled.",
    "sourcePage": 219
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Innocence of the Child's Heart",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "The ritual allows a magi to find the toy of a child who has carried it and reflect that child's own innocence upon themselves.",
    "vitaeCost": 2,
    "costText": "2 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "This ritual requires that the vampire who casts it be carrying a child's toy, and for as long as she is carrying it outwardly visible on her person the ritual remains in effect.",
    "castingDuration": "3 minutes as the magi's aura slowly becomes perfectly innocent",
    "successText": "Once the ritual is complete, the magi's aura is cleansed of all black veins, frightful emotions, and intimidating vampiric countenance. Instead, the vampire is felt to be spiritually and behaviorally innocent. Attempts to detect or treat the magi as anything other than innocent must gain more successes than the vampire gets on a Charisma Subterfuge roll, and must have some reason in the scene to even consider them otherwise before the attempt can be made. Regardless, the magi's aura will always show as genuinely finnocent' and perfect and calm.",
    "sourcePage": 219
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Mirror Wards",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "grand way to protect a magi's dwelling. Not a mad house but a place where your perceptions are turned back upon themselves to the point of not knowing where you came from and where you were going. Nothing looks the same, yet it all looks familiar.",
    "vitaeCost": 3,
    "willpowerCost": 1,
    "costText": "3 Vitae + 1 Willpower",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "As long as the glyphs are unbroken/intact. Once any glyph is broken, it and no others will work in that room. Glyphs may not be repaired, they must all be wiped away clean and the ritual created from new again.",
    "castingDuration": "A few moments to use chalk to mark glyphs of warding on the backs of all mirrors within a single home/dwelling.",
    "successText": "Anyone, other than the magi or those holding an item marked with the magi's glyph, acting within the area of the dwelling and mirrors are affected. All roll involving any Mental Attributes are made at a -2. And intruders become utterly lost unless they make an Intelligence + Investigation roll.",
    "sourcePage": 220
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Mirrorstep",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "Remaining after death is truly no more than a reflection of the mortal you once were. This ritual gives a magi power over their own reflected self. This ritual requires a ring with an emerald in it and a room with at least I mirror of hand size or larger. The mirror ripples, and the emerald glows, as this ritual is performed.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "I Scene. If anyone looks into a mirror you are seen within, they can say your name three times, which pulls you from the mirror. It must be your birth name, not a nickname or alternate identity.",
    "castingDuration": "The time it takes to touch your emerald ring to the mirror.",
    "successText": "When you step into a mirror, you can be seen in the mirror as wherever you might be in the room were you still there. But, in fact, you are no longer in the room — you are only in the mirror. You can still affect anyone reflected in the same mirror as you as if you were there with them, though they cannot affect you. If there are multiple mirrors in the room then maybe you can move into one and out of the other (back into the actual room).\n\nThe magi may spend 1 Willpower to take one person with them into the min-or. Simple items carried stay with the magi or person taken it. If the items are dropped, they are left in the actual room (not within the mirror) on the surface they were dropped upon. If the magi lets go of the person while in the mirror, the person is immediately 'reflected' back into the actual room. If the magi Burns a dot of Willpower, they may let go of the person in the mirror, and without another magi or emerald ring, they are trapped in there forever. Obtenebration The Obtenebration discipline grants its users power over darkness. The precise nature of the \"darkness\" invoked is a matter of debate. Some believe it to be shadows, while others, perhaps more correctly, believe the power grants a Vampire control over the black stains of her dark soul, allowing her to coax it tangibly forth. In any event, the effects of Obtenebration are terrifying, as waves of enveloping blackness roil out from the vampire, washing over their targets like an infernal tide. Blatant uses of this power are obvious breaches of the Masquerade.",
    "notes": "Note: Obtenebration may not be used in sunlight, direct or indirect it may be used in ambient sunlight. In addition it is weaker when used under water, while still adhering to the sunlight note above it's possible that Obtenebration may not function very well at all under water. Lastly, Obtenebration may also not be used in total darkness — there must be some source of light to cast at least a faint shadow. Thus Obtenebration may not be used inside a Shroud of Night affected area. If the ritual has already been started when the area is plunged into utter darkness, then it will still go into effect as normal.\n\nNote: Cameras will rarely if never pick up on shadows of Obtenebration, and even then they are likely hard to discern. Any supernatural awareness powers, however, have an excellent chance at detecting a shadow under the sway of Obtenebration.",
    "sourcePage": 220
  },
  {
    "group": "Noctis",
    "name": "Oblivion's Sight",
    "category": "Dark Reflections",
    "flavor": "The vampire closes their eyes. Upon opening them, the irises of their eyes are black against the white of their sclera, and they can now see clearly within pitch blackness - including those of supernatural origin. This blood ritual may be used in utter darkness.",
    "vitaeCost": 1,
    "costText": "1 Vitae",
    "dicePoolText": "None",
    "actionText": "<insert how to use the blood in a ritual>>",
    "duration": "Instant",
    "castingDuration": "Instant (A mere blink of the eye after the blood is used)",
    "sourcePage": 221
  },
